You are completely right. We created all those powerful backend methods in the VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java file, but we never actually exposed them to the internet through the Controller! Because of that, your React frontend will get a 404 Not Found error when it tries to fetch the data.
Here are the exact REST API endpoints you need to add to your controller (likely named VoucherPostingController.java or JournalController.java).
Just copy and paste these right inside your @RestController class:
```java
    // 1. Get Summary of all requests for the main DataGrid
    @GetMapping("/all-requests-summary")
    public ResponseEntity<List<Map<String, Object>>> getAllRequestsSummary() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getAllRequestsSummary());
    }

    // 2. Get the specific Batch & Origin Voucher Details for the top header cards
    @GetMapping("/batch-summary/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> getBatchSummaryDetails(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getBatchSummaryDetails(batchId));
    }

    // 3. Get the dynamic Stepper array (L1, L2, L3, etc.) based on the Workflow Code
    @GetMapping("/workflow/steps/{workflowCode}")
    public ResponseEntity<List<Map<String, Object>>> getWorkflowSteps(@PathVariable String workflowCode) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getWorkflowSteps(workflowCode));
    }

    // 4. Get the full Action History (who approved/rejected and their remarks)
    @GetMapping("/workflow-history/{batchId}")
    public ResponseEntity<List<Map<String, Object>>> getWorkflowHistory(@PathVariable String batchId) {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getWorkflowHistory(batchId));
    }

    // 5. Get Active Roles mapping to display proper names instead of role IDs (e.g., "Reviewer" instead of "9002")
    @GetMapping("/active-roles")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, String>> getActiveRoles() {
        return ResponseEntity.ok(voucherRequestService.getActiveRoles());
    }

```
Once you paste these into your Controller and restart your Spring Boot application, your new React Status screen will instantly light up with all the correct workflow states, the mini-stepper, and the full history timeline!
